ouple examples here so this  first one you can see x squared is  positive it comes first and that tells  us it's going to be opening in the x  axis direction so what we're going to do  is we're going to see that the a value  is actually 3 because the square root of  9 is 3 and so from the center we're  going to go right 3 left 3 those are  going to be our vertices right there  this is our B squared the square root of  16 is 4 so we're going to be going 4 in  the Y direction  okay one two three four and we're gonna  be drawing this dotted rectangle and all  this does is it just helps us to find  the asymptotes they go through the  corners of the rectangle also through  the center point and it give us a sense  of how wide or narrow the hyperbola is  so this hyperbola is going to be opening  like this getting closer and closer to  the asymptotes but it's not going to  cross them it just gives us a sense of  how wide it is now you can check out  I'll have a link to one of my other  videos where I go more into how to find  the equations of the asymptotes and the  foci and all that other good stuff about  hyperbolas but for this video I just  want to show you you know the direction  because that's the question that  students often have so in this last  example notice that the Y squared is  positive that tells us it's going to  open in the y axis Direction vertically  and the a squared is 4 so from the  center we're going to go up two and down  two and in the x direction we're going  to go right 1 and left 1 because the  square root of 1 is 1 and that just  allows us to draw this you know dotted  or dashed rectangle it's just invisible  it's just imaginary we're just using it  as a guide to draw our asymptotes and  you can see it's going to be opening  like this ok and that's it so I hope  this video helped you to understand how  to work with hyper beause a little bit  you know easier go ahead and check out  the other video I did on hyperbolas and  some of the other conic 